# Dashboard — current usage
This skill vendors upstream `uppinote20/claude-dashboard` v1.34.0 (MIT).
`NOTICE` and `UPSTREAM.sha256` record provenance, version, byte-level binding,
and our changes. This entry point defines when to enable it, what to inspect,
and the boundaries of its responsibility.
## Off by default
Nothing is installed until the user asks, and nothing repairs itself unless the user
opts in. A repository's decision to use jstack project checks never enables this.
When the user asks to turn the statusline on or change its layout, including
invoking this skill directly as `/jstack:dashboard`, run the `dashboard-setup` command
in Claude Code. It is not named `dashboard` because a command with a skill's name is
hidden behind the skill.
```
/jstack:dashboard-setup # Choose display mode, widgets, and language; install
/jstack:dashboard-repair # Check health; repair only what this plugin owns
export JSTACK_DASHBOARD_STATUSLINE=1 # Optional: enable session-start recovery for one environment
```
Setup writes `~/.claude/claude-dashboard.local.json`, then runs
`scripts/install-statusline.mjs install`. That script finds the installed package
from host installation metadata rather than a fixed marketplace or plugin name,
and confirms, through the installed shim, which build a render will load before it
writes `settings.json`; without a resolvable build it exits 1 and writes nothing.
Automatic recovery at session start is a separate opt-in per config directory,
recorded by setup or `install-statusline.mjs optin --auto-repair on`. The environment
variable enables it for one environment, and `JSTACK_DASHBOARD_STATUSLINE=0` disables it.
A healthy check writes nothing and prints nothing.
**Changes can be reverted.** The installer saves a `settings.json` backup before
writing. Repair fills an empty slot or fixes this plugin's own value, never replaces
a value the user set, and replaces another tool's value only after the user allows
that identity. Setup replaces a user value only after the user agrees to
`--replace`. An unparsable file is left untouched.
`references/recovery.md` is the single description of why a status line
disappears: the cause types reported by `status`, how to read that report, the
repair exit codes, every opt-in setting, keeping a watcher running, and the steps
for moving an existing machine to a new version. Read it before diagnosing a lost
or wrong status line.
When disabled, this skill does nothing. That is the normal default until setup
is requested. This entry configures Claude Code. The package selects a separate
Codex entry for its native `/statusline` and `tui.status_line` configuration;
the Claude installer and widget renderer do not configure the Codex footer, and
an existing Codex layout is preserved.
## What to inspect
| Dimension | Question |
|---|---|
| Context | How much capacity remains in this session? |
| Cost | How much has been spent, and how does it compare with today's budget? |
| Usage limits | How much of the 5-hour and 7-day allowance is used? When does it reset? |
| Other CLIs | Codex, Gemini, Antigravity, and z.ai limits |
`references/reading.md` explains the values and the 38 widgets.
`/jstack:check-usage` performs a one-time query without a statusline.
`/jstack:dashboard-alias` adds the `check-ai` shell function for that query.
## Emoji handling
`jstack` ships `block_emoji.py`, while the dashboard emits emoji in its statusline.
They do not mechanically conflict: the hook examines `PreToolUse` inputs, and
statusline output is not a tool call (measured).
Organization policy also covers output, so an emoji-free configuration has been
measured. Follow **Using it without emoji** in `references/reading.md`; model,
context, cost, and limits remain visible. Setup offers that layout as `noemoji`. That reference also lists measured emoji counts
for the default modes.
## Boundary with workspace
Both can answer availability questions, so ownership must remain explicit.
| Subject | Owner | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| gws OAuth token expiry | **workspace** | Account and authentication layer |
| Claude/Codex/Gemini usage, limits, and cost | **dashboard** | CLI runtime layer |
| Statusline display | **dashboard** | |
Answering the other skill's questions creates competing sources of truth.
See `references/boundary.md` for evidence and the decision procedure.
## Editing source and bundle together
`scripts/` contains the source; `dist/` is the committed bundle. Keep them aligned.
```
npm ci && npm run build # Rebuild dist
```
The build is byte-reproducible: the upstream commit, an isolated copy, and our
copy produced identical hashes. The contract suite checks reproducibility by
exit status. Changing only source or only the bundle makes this check fail.
When `node` is unavailable, this axis records its name and fails instead of
silently disappearing.
## Updating upstream
Follow the `upstream` skill: reacquire upstream, compare with `UPSTREAM.sha256`,
reapply local changes, and update the version and manifest together. `NOTICE`
records each local change and its reason. Identity-constant and build checks catch
omitted local changes.
## Explicit limitations
- We do not measure widget values. The contract checks import integrity,
reproducibility, identity constants, live hook and installer behavior, and one
sample render through an installed shim laid out like the built package. Upstream
vitest checks widget values (25 files), and runs only when `node` is available.
- Upstream vitest is load-sensitive. Three CLI shell test groups intermittently
fail at the default 5-second timeout (48/48 passed in isolation with 30 seconds).
That instability is not detection power, so our contract does not depend on it.
- Other CLI limits come from those CLIs' configuration files. A missing file or
format change silences only its widget; it is not a dashboard-wide failure.
